│ patch_name               │ exact_shared_broadcast                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The prior approach used a greedy multi-source attachment rule: at each iteration it       │
│                          │ selected the currently cheapest destination branch and attached it to the partially built │
│                          │ tree. Its key parameters were implicit: edge `cost` was the sole optimization weight, all │
│                          │ `num_partitions` were duplicated over the same route, and destination selection was based │
│                          │ only on immediate incremental path cost. This can miss globally cheaper shared relay      │
│                          │ structures.                                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new algorithm uses an exact directed Steiner-tree dynamic program when the number of  │
│                          │ unique destinations is at most `EXACT_TERMINAL_LIMIT = 11`. It minimizes the total cost   │
│                          │ of the union of broadcast links, allowing destinations to share low-cost prefixes and     │
│                          │ intermediate relay networks. The dynamic program evaluates all terminal subsets, merges   │
│                          │ compatible subtrees, and relaxes routes through the directed cloud graph. For larger      │
│                          │ destination sets, it uses a scalable shared-tree greedy fallback with terminal-leaf       │
│                          │ pruning. Paths are then extracted from the selected shared topology and replicated across │
│                          │ partitions, preserving the original input/output interface.                               │
