                        Patch Metadata - Gen 12/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_wavefront                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ This version replaces incremental destination attachment with a directed Steiner-tree     │
│                          │ wavefront algorithm. It models broadcast links as shared infrastructure: an edge is paid  │
│                          │ for once in the union, while any number of destination partitions can reuse it.           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ For modest destination sets, it uses subset dynamic programming combined with reverse     │
│                          │ multi-source Dijkstra relaxation. Each DP state finds the lowest-cost topology rooted at  │
│                          │ a node that can reach a subset of destinations. States can either merge two independently │
│                          │ delivered destination groups at a relay, or extend through a low-cost outgoing network    │
│                          │ path. This explicitly discovers economical relay clouds and shared backbone paths that    │
│                          │ greedy attachment orders can miss.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ For very large destination sets, it falls back to a shared-union heuristic with iterative │
│                          │ pruning and rerouting, preserving scalability. The final selected topology is converted   │
